Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah's torrential rains and floods resulted in 132 fatalities and left 176 individuals unaccounted for. According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP, the severe weather conditions demolished over 15,000 homes and forced 78,000 people to seek refuge in government-run temporary shelters. DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda confirmed, “We have 132 confirmed dead and another 176 missing,” while noting that relief efforts have been bolstered by the army, navy, and air force.

As the cyclone moved north toward India, the Sri Lankan government urged international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate funds to support nearly half a million people impacted by the calamity. Concurrently, new landslides occurred in Kandy, 115 km (70 miles) east of Colombo, inundating several sections of the primary access road, and worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo.

India promptly responded, with Prime Minister Narendra Modi expressing his condolences to the people of Sri Lanka via a post on X, mourning the cyclone's victims and reaffirming India’s commitment to aiding its "closest maritime neighbour." India swiftly dispatched relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment, including two aircraft loaded with relief items and provisions from an Indian warship docked in Colombo for a goodwill visit.
