As 2025 draws to a close, Vietnam may still face its 16th storm of the year, according to Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien. The year has been marked by highly atypical weather patterns and natural disasters, resulting in significant damage across the country, as highlighted by Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department.

The East Sea has experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression activity, reaching the highest level since Vietnam began record-keeping in 1961. By the end of November, 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, surpassing the previous high of 20 set in 2017. Notably, Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerged in June, an occurrence not seen in over four decades, and Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts surpassing this level, marking it as the most powerful storm ever recorded in the East Sea.

The year also witnessed extraordinary rainfall, with the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recording 1,739 mm within a single day in October. The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is evaluating this figure for possible acknowledgment as one of the greatest 24-hour rainfall totals ever documented worldwide.

Floods in 2025 were notably extensive and intense, with river levels reaching unprecedented heights across numerous areas, from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands and the lower Mekong Delta. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding, including the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, which surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

Deputy Director Hoang Duc Cuong observed that the array of atypical occurrences in Vietnam and globally during 2025 indicates a shift in conventional flood patterns. The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change.
