**Sri Lanka Battles Severe Flooding After Cyclone Ditwah**

In the wake of Cyclone Ditwah, Sri Lanka faces its most lethal natural disaster since 2017, with escalating floodwaters affecting large parts of the capital, Colombo. Authorities report that approximately 200,000 individuals have been rescued and relocated to shelters. Despite the cyclone having passed, intense rainfall upstream continues to cause flooding in low-lying areas adjacent to the Kelani River, leaving numerous families confined in their homes.

The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) has confirmed that the number of fatalities has risen to 159, with an additional 203 individuals still unaccounted for. Over 20,000 homes have been devastated, and 833,000 people require aid after being displaced by the floods. Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force are working alongside civilian workers and volunteers in relief efforts.

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced a state of emergency on Saturday, and international assistance has been sought. India has dispatched relief supplies, helicopters, and crews for rescue operations, while Japan has committed to sending a team to evaluate urgent needs and providing additional support.

Although the downpours have eased, numerous roads in the most severely impacted central province remain impassable. Access to electricity and running water is still lacking for about one-third of the nation due to downed power lines and flooded water treatment plants. Authorities have confirmed that power and communication services will resume in three days.

This cyclone marks the most severe flooding since June 2003, when 254 lives were lost, highlighting the ongoing challenges faced by Sri Lanka in managing natural disasters.
