data.fused_content:
The Labor government has announced a $200 million allocation for 96 projects nationwide, aimed at enhancing community resilience against extreme weather conditions. This funding is part of the Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a government initiative designed to bolster disaster resilience and mitigate risks. The DRF has already distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding across over 350 projects in its first two rounds, with almost 50% of these projects currently in pre-construction or construction-delivery stages, and 43 projects nearing completion or fully finished.

A significant portion of the $200 million, exceeding 50%, is earmarked for infrastructure projects, which include the construction of a cyclone shelter and evacuation center on Palm Island, off the coast of Queensland. This center will accommodate 800 people and is part of efforts to address the island's vulnerability to tropical cyclones and severe weather events. Additional initiatives include a $1.6 million infrastructure project to protect Townsvale Estate in South Australia and a $14.1 million investment to enhance stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, Central NSW. Furthermore, $895,000 is allocated to increase the capacity of Karajarri Rangers in northwest Western Australia, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats.

This third round of funding marks the DRF's commitment to making Australia more resilient, with the total investment reaching $600 million. The program benefits all 538 local government areas across states and territories. Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain emphasized the importance of the DRF, noting that Australians face annual challenges from floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which prompted the Albanese government to allocate up to $1 billion over five years to the fund.
