The death toll from floods and landslides on Sumatra island in Indonesia has risen to 708, according to the national disaster agency, with 504 individuals still unaccounted for. This marks a significant increase from the 604 fatalities reported on Monday. Initially, the death count was stated as 753, but it was later adjusted to 708 by Tuesday evening. The region has been severely impacted by intense monsoon rains and a rare tropical storm formed in the Malacca Strait, which has also affected parts of southern Thailand, resulting in 181 deaths there.

The monsoon season, known for heavy rains that can trigger landslides and floods, was exacerbated this year by the tropical storm, causing widespread devastation. In Indonesia, approximately 3.2 million people have been affected, with 2,600 injured and one million residents evacuated from high-risk zones. Communities across Aceh are at severe risk of food shortages and hunger if supply lines are not re-established within the next week. Prices for essentials like rice and vegetables have tripled, according to Islamic Relief, which is dispatching 12 tonnes of emergency food supplies.

Rescuers and aid teams are working to reach those affected, though their efforts are hindered by impassable roads and destroyed bridges. UNICEF noted that areas like Tapanuli Tengah in North Sumatra and Agam in Aceh are inaccessible by road. The Indonesian government announced the delivery of 34,000 tonnes of rice and 6.8 million litres of cooking oil to Aceh, North Sumatra, and West Sumatra.

The World Health Organization is deploying rapid response teams and critical supplies to the region, enhancing disease surveillance efforts. WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus emphasized that this situation highlights the increasing frequency and intensity of weather events due to climate change. Many survivors, currently housed in evacuation centers, recounted how torrential water suddenly inundated villages. Although rainfall has eased nationwide, landslide alerts remain in force across the hardest-hit central region.
