Numerous individuals have been displaced due to severe weather conditions that began at the end of last month, affecting regions across Southeast Asia. In Thailand, officials report that 170 individuals have died, while in Sri Lanka, the death toll has reached 410, with more than 499 individuals still unaccounted for. Sumatra, on Indonesia's western side, has been severely impacted, with certain regions experiencing rainfall exceeding 300 millimeters in just one day. This catastrophe has forced over 500,000 people in North Sumatra Province alone to seek refuge in emergency shelters.

The financial toll of the destruction is staggering. Authorities from a Thai financial research group estimate that the damage to commercial properties and agricultural land could amount to 25 billion baht, exceeding 780 million dollars. The tourism sector may suffer losses amounting to 10 billion baht, equivalent to over 310 million dollars. Thailand, one of the world's leading producers of natural rubber, has seen numerous producers experience flooding, with costs potentially surpassing 60 million dollars.

Specialists attribute the intensity of the recent rainfall to the La Nina climate phenomenon, characterized by cooler-than-usual waters off the coast of South America. Thon Thamrongnawasawat, an assistant professor at Kasetsart University in Bangkok, explains that the wind shifts course, directing moist air from South America's coastline toward Southeast Asia. Rising global temperatures have also elevated ocean heat, boosting vapor levels, which, coupled with La Nina, is expected to trigger significant disaster scenarios across various regions, notably affecting Vietnam, Malaysia, and the Philippines.

In response to the crisis, regional leaders are focusing on rapid aid deployment. On Monday, Indonesian President Prabowo Subianto visited communities affected by recent disasters, committing to rapidly supplying fuel and other critical necessities, alongside efforts to reinstate power to regions left without electricity. On Sunday, Sri Lankan President Anura Kumara Dissanayake called for international assistance, stating, "This is the first occasion a disaster of this magnitude has affected the entire nation." The complete scope of the damage remains uncertain, but the disaster has severely impacted industries across all three nations, with numerous hotels and eateries nationwide experiencing similar repercussions.
