Unpredictable weather patterns have triggered an unusual occurrence in Korea, significantly impacting apple harvests. As global temperatures rise and rainfall intensifies, apples are struggling to survive. This phenomenon, referred to as "yeolgwa," involves the rapid uptake of water by the fruit, which the skin cannot accommodate swiftly, causing the apples to split open.

The impact of extreme weather on Korean apples is particularly concerning because apples thrive in cooler climates, ideally when temperatures range from 45 to 52 degrees Fahrenheit. As countries continue to rely on non-renewable energy sources, the emission of harmful gases exacerbates planetary warming, leading to extreme weather events that threaten agricultural stability.

For farmers in South Jeolla Province, this situation has been devastating. Apple farmer Yoon Young-hwan reported that last year, about 80 out of 100 apples split open. Despite efforts to prevent it, this year approximately 60% of his apples cracked. Yoon expressed frustration, noting that switching to sub-tropical fruits is both costly and time-consuming, especially as the country imports apples. He warned that the entire apple industry could face collapse if these challenges persist.
