The World Meteorological Organization (WMO), under the United Nations, is advocating for the enhancement of global monitoring and prediction systems to better warn individuals, particularly in developing countries, about severe weather conditions. WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo emphasized at the UN's yearly conference in Geneva that "Early warning means early action." This initiative is crucial as a shift in the global climate has led to more frequent and severe occurrences of extreme weather events, such as hurricanes, flooding, wildfires, and glacier collapses.

The WMO has noted that almost 50% of countries, especially developing nations, face heightened exposure to these climatic phenomena due to inadequate alert systems. Over the past five decades, climate-related incidents have resulted in the deaths of more than 2 million individuals. The agency expresses concern over insufficient safeguards and asserts that implementing early warning systems could avert most fatalities attributed to weather conditions.

Despite advancements and a growing adoption of early warning systems among nations, significant efforts are still needed. The WMO highlighted that while more countries are adopting these systems, half are only engaging with their fundamental features, and 16% are operating below even these basic levels. A preemptive alert mechanism offers additional time for evacuation and readiness in anticipation of severe weather incidents, providing individuals with the chance to strengthen their surroundings or evacuate safely.

The WMO is striving to enhance recognition of the underuse of early warning systems for extreme weather occurrences. With the establishment of operational systems in additional countries, the preservation of lives will increase. The organization emphasized that climate-related weather events are on the rise, noting that "impacts are escalating as weather patterns grow more severe," yet further efforts are necessary to address these challenges.
