**Victoria Intensifies Support for Bushfire-Affected Families and Farmers**

Government officials at both federal and Victorian levels are intensifying their support for families and primary producers impacted by the January bushfires in Victoria, building upon previous disaster recovery strategies. Premier Jacinta Allan announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their main residence will be eligible for grants up to $52,250. "The devastation wrought by these fires has been beyond measure for countless individuals across Victoria – which is why we are providing additional financial and psychological aid to alleviate some of the challenges faced by our farmers and regional communities," Allan stated.

Kristy McBain, the Federal Minister for Emergency Management, emphasized the Albanese government's collaborative efforts with Victoria during the recovery phase. "We want the people of Victoria to be assured that the Albanese government supports you not only in responding to these fires but also throughout the recovery process," McBain affirmed.

Support programs are centered on providing farmers and livestock with necessary access. In response to numerous setbacks affecting agricultural zones, the Victorian government is enhancing mental health and business assistance for farmers. The "Look Over the Farm Gate" initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, is scheduled to commence ahead of time on Jan. 16. This initiative will provide grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities specifically in farming areas to support mental health and community recovery.

Steve Dimopoulos, the Acting Victorian Minister for Agriculture, stated that the strategy aims to address both safety and welfare concerns. "In collaboration with Agriculture Victoria and the Fire Incident Controller, our efforts are focused on simplifying the process for farmers to monitor their animals' well-being, as we understand the distressing nature of this period," Dimopoulos added.

As access to affected regions improves, insurers have commenced claim processing and are dispatching personnel to these areas. The Insurance Council of Australia (ICA) reports that insurers have received 1,386 claims since January 7, spanning home, commercial, motor, and business interruption insurance. Insurers have initiated the processing of these claims and are dispatching personnel to impacted regions as access becomes available.

To facilitate public donations, the Victorian government is initiating the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal. The government has assured that all contributions will be allocated to communities impacted by the fires, with the state covering administrative expenses. Vicki Ward, the Victorian Minister for Natural Disaster Recovery, stated that the appeal aims to distribute funds based on priorities set by local communities, in collaboration with charities and community organizations.

In response to ongoing bushfires and extreme weather, general insurers are activating catastrophe protocols. IAG's Major Event Response Teams have initiated visits to properties in affected neighborhoods to conduct evaluations and undertake urgent repairs. IAG has established a mobile claims unit in Seymour to assist patrons of its affiliated brands, such as NRMA Insurance, CGU, WFI, Rollin RACQ Insurance, and RACV. CEO Steve Johnston stated that once emergency authorities provide clearance, mobile disaster hubs, along with customer support teams, assessors, and builders, are ready to deploy into affected areas.

Health funds are revising their policies for clients in impacted areas. HCF is providing qualified members in rural and remote locations with complimentary 15-minute online GP consultations via its collaboration with GP2U, ensuring appointments are accessible every day of the week. Additionally, HCF has rolled out a relief initiative for members affected by the Victorian bushfires and other severe weather occurrences, offering eligible members a premium waiver of up to three months and the ability to pause coverage for up to two years, with participation in the HealthyMinds Check-in program.
