data.fused_content:
In response to the region's harsh cold and powerful winds, emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich, triggering an Extreme Weather Response. In Saanich, the shelter at 3703 St. Aidan’s St., managed by Our Place Society with financial backing from the District of Saanich provided by BC Housing, offers 25 provisional winter shelter spots and 25 fixed sleeping accommodations. These Extreme Weather Response (EWR) beds are distinct from standard nightly shelter accommodations and are deployed when severe weather conditions arise, such as freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or snowfall.

The EWR spaces will be accessible as long as severe weather conditions fulfill provincial activation standards. In addition to the shelter at St. Aidan’s St., residents of Saanich have the option to utilize the shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals. Both shelters accommodate individuals of all genders and have a strict no-pet policy.

Saanich has also made daytime public warming facilities available at municipal recreation centers and the Saanich municipal hall during their standard hours of operation. The available facilities include Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The district notes that operational hours might fluctuate in response to severe weather, influenced by staffing availability.

In Victoria, the municipality reports the addition of 30 more shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative, provided by collaborators at the Salvation Army ARC. Overnight accommodations are accessible at 525 Johnson St., beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. Environment Canada forecasts indicate winds will increase to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight.
