**COLOMBO** — Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday as the death toll from floods and landslides caused by Cyclone Ditwah rose to 153, with 191 individuals still missing. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that over 20,000 homes were devastated, displacing 798,000 people who required aid and forcing 108,000 into government-run temporary shelters.

A passenger recounted from the hospital how navy sailors used ropes to guide them to the roof of a nearby house, above the floodwaters. A helicopter's initial rescue attempt was halted due to the risk of the rotor downdraft dislodging the roof, and the rescue was subsequently completed by naval boats.

In the central district of Badulla, roads remained impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country was without electricity or running water, as power lines collapsed and water purification facilities were inundated. "We lost two people in our village; others have taken refuge in a temple, and one house still standing," reported Saman Kumara from Maspanna, one of the hardest-hit areas in Badulla. "We can't leave the village, and no one can enter as all routes are obstructed by landslides. We lack food and are running out of clean water," he conveyed to the News Centre via telephone. Internet connections were also disrupted.

Cyclone Ditwah departed Sri Lanka on Saturday, heading towards neighboring India. In response, Chennai Airport in India cancelled 54 flights, with meteorologists predicting extremely heavy rainfall and strong winds for the next 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has called for international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate money to support affected communities. An Indian warship, on a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, contributed its supplies to assist the victims.
