data.fused_content:
Alicante is poised to face severe weather conditions precisely one year after catastrophic floods claimed 220 lives. Tourists bound for the Costa Blanca have been cautioned due to an unusual weather alert predicting heavy rainfall for this popular vacation destination, which experienced lethal flooding in the previous year.

A seldom-seen red alert has been declared for the southeastern coast of Spain, escalating the warnings released earlier this week and putting the region on high alert due to the impending "extraordinary danger." AEMET, Spain's meteorological authority, issued a warning on Thursday regarding the potential for significant rainfall in Alicante—a region that experienced devastating floods resulting in 220 fatalities last October. The measure will begin at 10 am local time on Friday.

The forecaster stated: "Significant and continuous rainfall is anticipated through Monday. These showers could lead to localized flash flooding in low-lying regions, streams, and gullies, elevating the potential danger level to high. Extraordinary danger is possible, with flooding and sudden flash floods likely. Heed the guidance of civil protection."

On Friday, AEMET escalated the weather warning to a red alert due to intense rainfall predicted in the entirety of Alicante and sections of Murcia. Meanwhile, Valencia's advisory has been raised to amber, anticipating up to 140 mm of rain within the forthcoming 12 hours.

The UK Foreign Office has advised British travelers heading to Spain to expect severe weather conditions and possible travel interruptions, stating: "Severe weather and travel disruption. Disruptions to travel are likely." They urged travelers to adhere to the guidance from local officials and keep an eye on regional weather reports. Before embarking on your journey, be sure to review any communications from your airline or travel provider.

Jerome Torres, a British resident, recounted the terrifying experience of listening to his neighbors' last cries as the devastating floods swept through. He revealed that he heard their screams just before they perished. Torres described the Spanish city as resembling a scene from a "horror film," ravaged by torrential floods, which left homes and businesses in ruins and streets blanketed in a dense coat of mud.

This event marks the most severe flood disaster in Europe since 1967, a year when Portugal saw the loss of at least 500 lives.
