data.fused_content:
A Senate inquiry has been backed by Labor, the coalition, and the Greens to investigate the catastrophic algal bloom that has claimed the lives of tens of thousands of marine creatures since its detection near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March. The bloom has spread along the coastline, prompting a $28 million investment package co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments. This package aims to support clean-up, recovery, research, and industry assistance efforts.

Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked that the widespread support for the inquiry indicated the issue transcended political boundaries. "Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics," Cadell said. The investigation provides an opportunity to fully understand the tangible consequences of this ecological catastrophe and evaluate the adequacy of the existing disaster relief funding framework.

The unprecedented algal bloom has left the community uncertain about its duration and potential additional consequences. Premier Peter Malinauskas announced that the SA government would match the federal contribution with a $14 million commitment. The funding package includes $8.5 million for a coastal science and research network designed to enhance the early detection and monitoring of harmful algal bloom species. Additionally, a $3 million rapid assessment will evaluate the effects of the bloom on fish stocks and fisheries.

A newly established $2 million national testing lab in South Australia will eliminate the need to send samples to New Zealand for analysis. This initiative, crafted by federal Environment Minister Murray Watt, aims to improve the ability to promptly identify and track harmful algal bloom species. The algal bloom underscores the urgency for climate action, as noted by various officials.
