**UK Faces Severe Wheat Shortfall Due to Climate Change**

A report has revealed that since 2020, the UK has lost a year's supply of bread due to harvests devastated by severe weather conditions. Specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) estimated that the shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat output could produce over 4 billion loaves of bread, equating to a year's worth of supply. This decade has witnessed two of the three most disastrous harvests on record, with 2020 being the most severe, followed by the third worst in 2024.

The intensification of climate breakdown has led to droughts and floods, resulting in significant challenges for farmers. This year, crops have endured severe challenges due to one of the hottest and driest springs and summers ever recorded. The outlook for 2025 points to another challenging year for agricultural yields, with farmers facing unprecedented heatwaves, arid conditions, and restrictions on irrigation.

Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming at the ECIU, commented, “This is the reality of farming amid a shifting climate. Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production, a trend expected to worsen with the escalation of extreme weather in the years to come.” The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024.

Colin Chappell, an arable farmer from Lincolnshire, stated, “I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible. I'm seeking additional assistance to manage these challenges and strengthen my business's resilience, yet the closure of green farming initiatives in England means that aid is currently unavailable to me. Reopening these programs must be prioritized if farmers are to sustain the cultivation of wheat and other essential crops for the Great British public.”

Millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat to produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits, with last autumn's wheat imports being twice the five-year average. Global wheat shortages are intensifying supply challenges internationally, as various countries face their own issues with conflicts and adverse weather conditions reducing crop outputs.
