Pakistani rescue officials report that above-average monsoon rains since June 26 have resulted in at least 225 deaths and over 500 injuries nationwide. Specialists assert that the escalation of extreme weather phenomena in the area is fueled by climate change. A sudden cloudburst on Monday triggered floods and landslides, leaving over 200 local tourists stranded when a major highway became obstructed. Army helicopters facilitated the evacuation process for tourists, including women and children.

Numerous vehicles have been buried beneath landslide debris, prompting rescuers to deploy heavy equipment to locate missing tourists and inhabitants. Three bodies have been recovered amid the debris. The National Disaster Management Authority recently released a revised flood warning, advising against travel to the northern regions due to the risk of landslides and road obstructions.

Authorities issued a warning earlier this month, indicating that the potential for experiencing severe weather conditions similar to the 2022 floods—which inundated a third of the nation and resulted in 1,737 fatalities—cannot be dismissed. Faizullah Faraq, a spokesperson for the government in the Gilgit-Baltistan area, highlighted the ongoing challenges posed by these extreme weather events.
