Mrutyunjay Mohapatra, the Director of the India Meteorological Department (IMD), has indicated that while light rain is expected to persist throughout the coming week, there is no forecast for heavy, widespread rainfall in the short term. Mohapatra highlighted the potential emergence of a new low-pressure system in the Bay of Bengal around September 20, which could lead to wind gusts, though a cyclone is currently considered improbable. He noted that the forecast is expected to become clearer in the coming days, as the trajectory and strength of the system are contingent upon evolving meteorological conditions.

Mohapatra assured that there is no imminent threat of cyclones or severe weather conditions to Odisha. Although the overall rainfall is diminishing, the IMD cautions that localized heavy showers and thunderstorms remain a threat, particularly in regions susceptible to flooding and low-lying zones. The earlier low-pressure system, which caused heavy rainfall in the coastal and western districts, has now moved towards north Telangana and Vidarbha, resulting in a steady decrease in rainfall throughout the state.

Authorities remain vigilant, keeping a watchful eye on the Bay of Bengal for any potential events that might affect both coastal and inland areas. The IMD will continue to monitor the situation closely, providing updates as more information becomes available.
