**TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif.** — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The blaze swiftly grew to cover 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. There were no immediate reports of injuries or fatalities.

The fire, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, has consumed over 19 square miles across various areas. At least five residences, a combination of standalone and mobile homes, were ablaze in the rural area. On Tuesday evening, efforts were made in a desperate attempt to keep the blaze from spreading from the neighboring house. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire. A group of seven individuals hastily relocated large tree branches away from the building and shoveled sand onto the flames, striving to prevent the fire from spreading, until firefighters arrived.

Fire authorities have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft. According to CalFire, this fire is among over a dozen that ignited across California on Tuesday, originating from a lightning strike. Containment efforts had not been successful by Wednesday morning.

Chinese Camp emerged as a settlement for Chinese miners who had relocated from a neighboring camp after being expelled. During the Gold Rush, these miners encountered widespread discrimination, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry. Initially named Camp Washington, the town was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers.
