The Environment Agency has successfully completed 151 flood management projects across England from July 2024 to 2025, significantly enhancing flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses nationwide. These initiatives, which include the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, were implemented following Labour's rise to power last July. Among the completed projects are the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, protecting 2,904 local properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, safeguarding 1,526 properties. Additionally, the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme in Essex, which renewed three kilometers of tidal defenses, will protect more than 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

The Government has committed to boosting flood defense spending, pledging £2.65 billion over the next two years until April 2026, marking a £250 million increase over the previous allocation. This investment is part of a broader promise of £7.9 billion in flood defenses from 2026 to 2036, totaling over £10.5 billion over 12 years from 2024. Ministers highlight that this investment will benefit more than 890,000 properties, protect businesses, safeguard farmland, and support the Government's plan to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029. The initiatives are also expected to drive economic growth, create jobs, and build new homes for future generations.

The Environment Agency notes that the number of properties at risk from flooding in the UK is increasing due to rising sea levels, more volatile rainfall patterns, outdated flood defenses, and increased development. Currently, 6.3 million properties in England are at risk, with estimates suggesting this could rise to around eight million by 2050. Caroline Douglass, executive director of floods and coastal risk management at the Environment Agency, emphasized the devastating impact of flooding on communities and the need for continued collaboration with the government to deliver long-term flood investment, ensuring protection for the most vulnerable communities.
