**Flash Floods in Gansu Province Claim Lives, Trigger Nationwide Rescue Efforts**

In northwestern China, flash floods in Gansu province have resulted in at least 10 fatalities and 33 individuals reported missing. The flooding began on August 7, with relentless downpours causing sudden inundations. By 3:30 pm (07:30 GMT) on August 8, the death toll had reached 10, with 33 individuals unaccounted for, according to state broadcaster CCTV.

The floods have been part of a broader pattern of extreme weather affecting China, with torrential rains and landslides triggering mass evacuations in northern China. Since late July, severe downpours and consequent flooding have claimed the lives of at least 60 individuals in northern China, including regions such as Beijing.

Chinese President Xi Jinping has called for "all-out" rescue efforts, demanding the "utmost effort" to locate and save those who are missing. Xi has ordered all regions to "resolutely overcome complacency" and strengthen efforts to identify risks associated with these frequent extreme weather events, which meteorologists attribute to climate change.

Images released by the Gansu authorities depicted thoroughfares blanketed in silt and sizable rocks. Video footage posted by Chinese firefighting officials on Weibo showed rescuers leading villagers through turbulent grey floodwaters.

In recent weeks, China has experienced heavy rainfall in both its northern and southern regions, prompting flood warnings across 11 provinces. The situation underscores the increasing challenges posed by climate change-induced extreme weather conditions.
